WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:06.301
Marius slowly climbed the steps to his apartment, his heart heavy with disappointment.

2
00:00:06.302 --> 00:00:12.213
"Monsieur Marius, you seem sad," said a voice behind him. "What’s wrong?"

3
00:00:12.214 --> 00:00:20.443
At the top of the stairs, he turned around and found the older Jondrette girl. "Nothing’s wrong," he said harshly. "Leave me alone."

4
00:00:20.444 --> 00:00:26.126
"No, you gave me something to eat; now let me help you," she pleaded.

5
00:00:26.127 --> 00:00:34.673
"I help my father all the time. Do you need me to deliver a message, or follow someone, or find out an address?"

6
00:00:34.674 --> 00:00:42.094
Marius realized that she might be useful after all. "You brought that old man and his daughter here. Do you know where they live?"

7
00:00:42.095 --> 00:00:51.689
"No, I met them at the church, but I can find out the address of the beautiful young lady if you wish."

8
00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:59.007
The bitterness with which she said "beautiful young lady" made Marius cringe, but he needed her assistance.

9
00:00:59.008 --> 00:01:01.266
"Yes, please do that for me."

10
00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:04.665
Marius entered his apartment and dropped into a chair.

11
00:01:04.666 --> 00:01:11.657
So much had happened since that morning. His angel had reappeared and then disappeared and now—

12
00:01:11.658 --> 00:01:15.570
"I tell you . . . I’m sure I recognized him!"

13
00:01:15.571 --> 00:01:20.990
Monsieur Jondrette’s angry voice from the room next door cut suddenly through Marius’ thoughts.

14
00:01:20.991 --> 00:01:25.808
Marius climbed upon the table once more and peered through his spy hole.

15
00:01:25.809 --> 00:01:31.641
Monsieur Jondrette had just come in and was taking off the coat that Monsieur Leblanc had given him.

16
00:01:31.642 --> 00:01:39.324
"Are you positive it’s the same man?" asked Madame Jondrette. "Why didn’t he recognize you?"

17
00:01:39.325 --> 00:01:41.967
Monsieur Jondrette stroked his beard.

18
00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:50.788
"Because I am in disguise, but even after all these years, I knew him immediately and the girl too.

19
00:01:50.789 --> 00:01:54.046
Don’t you remember that girl who used to live with us?"

20
00:01:54.047 --> 00:02:00.880
" That girl! " Madame Jondrette’s voice was a mixture of surprise, rage, and

21
00:02:00.881 --> 00:02:10.954
hatred. "Impossible! My daughters go barefoot and don’t have a decent dress between them while her outfit must be worth at least two hundred

22
00:02:10.955 --> 00:02:14.627
francs!" "I’m telling you again that I recognized the two of

23
00:02:14.628 --> 00:02:17.693
them." Madame Jondrette shook her head in

24
00:02:17.694 --> 00:02:27.678
disbelief. "But Cosette used to be so ugly! You’re saying she’s that horrible, beautiful young lady who looked at my girls with such

25
00:02:27.679 --> 00:02:31.575
pity? Oh, I’d like to stomp her heart

26
00:02:31.576 --> 00:02:36.381
out!" "We’ll do better than that, my dear. The old man will make us

27
00:02:36.382 --> 00:02:38.916
rich." Madame Jondrette looked

28
00:02:38.917 --> 00:02:45.821
puzzled. "It’s all arranged," said her husband. "While I was out, I talked to some men and bought

29
00:02:45.822 --> 00:02:50.400
this." He showed her a chisel, and then laid the blade in the

30
00:02:50.401 --> 00:02:55.162
fire. "It may come in handy later if he won’t

31
00:02:55.163 --> 00:03:03.725
cooperate." Marius could not believe what he was hearing. It appeared that Monsieur Leblanc—and perhaps Ursula too—was in grave

32
00:03:03.726 --> 00:03:07.067
danger. He slipped out of his apartment and went to the police

33
00:03:07.068 --> 00:03:12.392
station. "The chief of police isn’t in," said an office boy when Marius arrived at the

34
00:03:12.393 --> 00:03:16.138
station. "But you can speak to an inspector if it’s

35
00:03:16.139 --> 00:03:22.971
important." Marius nodded and the boy led him to an office, where a tall man was standing near the

36
00:03:22.972 --> 00:03:26.034
fire. "What do you want?" asked the

37
00:03:26.036 --> 00:03:29.384
inspector. "It’s a very urgent matter," said

38
00:03:29.385 --> 00:03:31.651
Marius. "Then you’d better speak

39
00:03:31.652 --> 00:03:37.360
quickly." Marius told the inspector how he’d overheard the plot to ambush Monsieur

40
00:03:37.361 --> 00:03:43.543
Leblanc. He didn’t know Leblanc’s real name or address, but he was sure that the old man was in

41
00:03:43.544 --> 00:03:49.114
danger. This was all going to take place at six o’clock in the old Gorbeau

42
00:03:49.115 --> 00:03:53.607
house. The inspector raised his eyebrows. "The old Gorbeau

43
00:03:53.608 --> 00:03:55.289
house?" "Are you familiar with

44
00:03:55.290 --> 00:04:00.185
it?" "Indeed I am." The inspector reached into a drawer and pulled out a

45
00:04:00.186 --> 00:04:04.185
pistol. "Take this and go back to spying from your

46
00:04:04.186 --> 00:04:12.011
room. My men and I will be hiding in the neighborhood. When the situation is at its most dangerous, fire the pistol and we’ll rush

47
00:04:12.012 --> 00:04:16.074
in." Marius took the gun and hid it in his

48
00:04:16.075 --> 00:04:25.908
waistcoat. "By the way," said the policeman, "if you need me before then, send a message to me here. My name is Inspector

49
00:04:25.909 --> 00:04:30.146
Javert." Marius went home and tiptoed into his

50
00:04:30.147 --> 00:04:33.821
apartment. There was only half an hour before Leblanc was due to

51
00:04:33.822 --> 00:04:39.908
return. As Marius climbed on top of his table, he could hear Monsieur Jondrette quizzing his

52
00:04:39.909 --> 00:04:44.321
daughters. "You saw the housekeeper go out, but what about the fellow next

53
00:04:44.322 --> 00:04:47.845
door?" "I heard him leave a while ago," said the older

54
00:04:47.846 --> 00:04:53.718
daughter. "He always goes out for his supper around this time and stays away for several

55
00:04:53.719 --> 00:04:57.059
hours." "You’d better check just to be

56
00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:05.210
sure." Marius dropped to his hands and knees and silently crawled under his bed. He’d just hidden himself when he saw his door

57
00:05:05.211 --> 00:05:10.070
open. "He’s gone out, Papa," said the older girl. "His key is in the

58
00:05:10.071 --> 00:05:13.051
door." "Go in anyway," called her

59
00:05:13.052 --> 00:05:19.861
father. The girl entered the room with a candle and walked straight toward the bed. But she didn’t look under

60
00:05:19.862 --> 00:05:25.528
it. Instead she headed for the mirror, where she gazed at herself and hummed a

61
00:05:25.529 --> 00:05:32.202
song. Meanwhile Marius was trembling under the bed. He couldn’t believe that she didn’t hear him

62
00:05:32.203 --> 00:05:36.940
breathing. "What are you doing over there?" called Monsieur Jondrette from next

63
00:05:36.941 --> 00:05:43.853
door. "I’m checking under all the furniture," said the daughter as she continued to arrange her hair in the

64
00:05:43.854 --> 00:05:47.502
mirror. "Come back here! It’s time for you to go

65
00:05:47.503 --> 00:05:51.337
out." The girl left and Marius crawled from under the bed.

66
00:05:51.338 --> 00:05:58.084
He dusted himself off and climbed the table just in time to hear Jondrette giving instructions to his daughters.

67
00:05:58.085 --> 00:06:05.067
They were to stand guard at each end of the street and come back immediately at the first sign of anything strange.

68
00:06:05.068 --> 00:06:09.472
The girls left and Jondrette began to pace the room.

69
00:06:09.473 --> 00:06:14.408
"Do you know what we need?" he said to his wife. "Two more chairs."

70
00:06:14.409 --> 00:06:18.242
"No problem. I’ll get them from the neighbor’s."

71
00:06:18.243 --> 00:06:26.559
Before Marius could hide under the bed, Madame Jondrette had entered his apartment. Fortunately she had not brought a candle.

72
00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:33.892
Marius flattened himself against the wall while she went directly to his chairs, picked them up, and left the room.

73
00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:38.892
Marius did not dare to breathe until she was safely on the other side of the wall.

74
00:06:38.893 --> 00:06:43.706
"Good!" said Monsieur Jondrette as he arranged the chairs facing each other.

75
00:06:43.706 --> 00:06:48.622
"Now take this lantern downstairs and wait for our guest."

76
00:06:48.623 --> 00:06:52.289
Marius peered around the Jondrettes’ darkened room.

77
00:06:52.290 --> 00:07:02.376
He noticed a pile of rope and some metal objects on the floor, but he didn’t recognize them for what they were: burglar’s tools.

78
00:07:02.377 --> 00:07:08.519
Jondrette pulled a long carving knife from a drawer in the table and ran his finger along the edge.

79
00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:13.606
Then he hid it away again and sat down in one of Marius’ chairs.

80
00:07:13.607 --> 00:07:21.519
For his part, Marius took the pistol from his pocket and cocked it. The gun made a distinct click.

81
00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:25.765
Jondrette started and half rose from his chair. "Who’s there?"

82
00:07:25.766 --> 00:07:35.316
Marius didn’t dare to move. Jondrette listened for a moment and then began to laugh.

83
00:07:35.317 --> 00:07:39.515
"What a fool I am! It’s just the wall creaking."

84
00:07:39.516 --> 00:07:42.753
Marius kept his pistol in his hand.

85
00:07:42.754 --> 00:07:49.611
A church clock struck six o’clock, and Marius heard the sound of a carriage arriving outside.

